</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we need to use the following equation:

ΔS = ΣnCpCpm * ln(T/T0) + ΣnΔH/T

where n is the number of moles of the substance, CpCpm is the heat capacity at constant pressure, T is the final temperature, and T0 is the initial temperature.

In this case, the reaction is:

3CO(g) + 1.5O2(g) → 3CO2(g)

Since the reaction is carried out at constant pressure and the temperature is constant (298 K), we can use the following equation:

ΔS = ΣnΔSf

where ΔSf is the standard free energy change of formation for each substance.

The standard free energy change of formation (ΔSf°) for CO2 is -215.1 J/mol·K, and for CO is -205.8 J/mol·K.

ΔS = [3(-215.1 J/mol·K)] - [3(-0.676 J/mol·K)] - [1.5(0.696 J/mol·K)]

ΔS = -645.3 J/mol·K + 2.028 J/mol·K - 1.044 J/mol·K

ΔS = -644.316 J/mol·K

The entropy change for the reaction is approximately -644.32 J/mol·K.
