","The properties of polymer-based smart materials can be tailored to respond to specific stimuli such as temperature, pH, or light by incorporating specific functional groups, monomers, or other components that can undergo structural or chemical changes upon exposure to these stimuli. Some of the common strategies include:

1. Temperature-responsive polymers: These polymers undergo changes in their physical properties (e.g., solubility, viscosity, elasticity) upon temperature changes. Examples include:
   a. Poly(N-isopropylacrylamide) (PNIPAM): This is a common thermos-cleumple polymer that undergoes a lower critical temperature (LCST) of around 25°C. Above this temperature, the polymer becomes hydrophobic and separates out, while below this temperature, it is hydrophilic and soluble in water. By changing the molecular weight, crosslinking density, or introducing functional groups, the LCST can be tuned.
   b. Poly(ethylene methacrylate) (PEMA): Similar to PNIPAM, PEMA has a lower clearing point (LCP) and can be used for temperature-sensitive applications.
   c. thermoamphphilic polymers: These polymers have both hydrophilic and hydrophobic groups, and their properties can be tuned to form micelles or micelles micelles at specific temperatures.

