   \[
   \text{div}(G') = D' + P_4 + P_5 + P_6 + P_7.
   \]
   Since \( G' \) is a conic, \( \text{div}(G') \) must consist of 8 points (counting multiplicities), but here it has degree 4 + deg(\( D' \)). For \( D' \) to be effective and \( \text{div}(G') \) to match, \( D' \) must include \( P_1, P_2, P_3 \), implying \( D' = D \).

4. **Conclusion:**
   No other effective divisor \( D' \) can satisfy \( D' \equiv D \) because \( G' \) would need to pass through additional points not in \( D \), which is impossible given the distinctness of the \( P_i \). Thus, \( D \) is not linearly equivalent to any other effective divisor.

\[
\boxed{D \text{ is not linearly equivalent to any other effective divisor.}}
\]
