<USER_REQUEST>
What is the magnetic field strength at the center of a circular coil with 100 turns that carries a current of 1 A and has a radius of 0.2 m?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The magnetic field strength \( B \) at the center of a circular coil is given by the formula:

\[
B = \frac{\mu_0}{2\pi r} \cdot I \cdot \sqrt{2}
\]

where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of free space, which is \( 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m/A} \).
- \( r \) is the radius of the coil.
- \( I \) is the current through the coil.

For a coil with \( N \) turns, the magnetic field strength is:

\[
B = \frac{N \mu_0}{2\pi r} \cdot I
\]

Substituting the given values:
- \( N = 100 \) turns
- \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \)
- \( r = 0.2 \, \text{m} \)
- \( \mu_0 = 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m/A} \)

\[
B = \frac{100 \times (4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m/A})}{2\pi \times 0.2 \, \text{m}} \cdot 1 \, \text{A}
\]

\[
B = \frac{400 \pi \times 10^{-7} \times 100}{2\pi \times 0.2} \, \text{T}
\]

\[
B = \frac{400 \times 10^{-7}}{0.4} \, \text{T}
\]

\[
B = 1000 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T}
\]

\[
B = 10^{-4} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil is \( \boxed{10^{-4} \, \text{T}} \).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

