","To find the percentage change in the closed-loop gain \( A_{fv} \), follow these steps:

1. **Calculate the initial closed-loop gain \( A_{fv1} \):**
   \[
   A_{fv1} = \frac{A_v}{1 + m_v} = \frac{100}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{100}{1.1} \approx 90.9 \text{ V/V}
   \]

2. **Calculate the new output voltage gain \( A_v' \):**
   The gain decreases by 6 dB, so the new gain is:
   \[
   A_v'' = A_w \times 10^{-6/10} = 100 \times 10^{-0.6} \approx 100 \times 0.21 \approx 21 \text{ V/V}
   \]

3. **Calculate the new closed-loop gain \( A_{fv2} \):**
   \[
   A_{fv2} = \frac{A_v''}{1 + m_w} = \frac{21}{1 + 0.1} = \frac{21}{1.1} \approx 19.09 \text{ V/V}
   \]

4. **Compute the percentage change in the closed-loop gain:**
   \[
   \text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{A_{ff2} - A_{ff1}}{A_{ff1}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{19.09 - 90.9}{90.9} \right) \times 100 \approx \left( \frac{-71.81}{90.9} \right) \times 100 \approx -79.1\%
   \]

