","The maximum percentage change in the wavelength of a 2.00 Å photon scattered by an electron occurs when the angle of scattering is 180° (180° scattering). This is because the momentum change is always greatest when the photon is scattered away the opposite direction. The momentum change in the photon is given by:

\[
\Delta p = \frac{hc}{\lambda_1} - \frac{hc}{\lambda_2}
\]

For a 2.00 Å photon, the momentum change is equal to the momentum of the electron. The momentum change in the electron is:

\[
\Delta p = p_e = \frac{m_e v}{2}
\]

The momentum change in the photon is:

\[
\Delta p = \frac{hc}{\lambda_1} - \frac{hc}{\lambda_2} = \frac{hc}{\lambda_1} - \frac{hc}{\lambda_2}
\]

For a 180° scattering, the momentum change in the photon is equal to twice the momentum of the photon:

\[
\Delta p = \frac{hc}{\lambda_1} - \frac{hc}{\lambda_2} = 2 \left( \frac{h_e}{m_e} \right)
\]

The momentum change in the photon is also equal to the momentum of the electron:

\[
\Delta p = \frac{hc}{\lambda_1} - \frac{hc}{\lambda_2} = p_e
\]

The momentum change in the electron is:

\[
p_e = \frac{m_e v}{2}
\]

For a 180° scattering, the momentum change in the photon is equal to twice the momentum of the photon:

\[
\frac{hc}{\lambda_1} - \frac{hc}{\lambda_2} = 2 \left( \frac{hc}{\lambda_1} \right)
\]

This implies:

\[
\frac{hc}{\lambda_2} = \lambda_1
\]

Thus, the final wavelength is:

\[
\lambda_2 = \frac{1}{2} \lambda_1
\]

The percentage change in the wavelength is:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\lambda_2 - \lambda_1}{\lambda_1} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{\frac{1}{2} \lambda_1 - \lambda_1}{\lambda_1} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{-\frac{1}{2} \lambda_1}{\lambda_1} \right) \times 100 = -50\%
\]

