<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To calculate the work done, we use the formula:

\[
W = F \cdot d \cdot \cos \theta
\]

Where:
- \( W = \text{work done} \)
- \( F = \text{force applied} \)
- \( d = \text{distance traveled} \)
- \( \theta = \text{angle between the force and displacement} \)

Substituting the given values:
\[
W = 60.0 \text{ N} \times 10.0 \text{ m} \times \cos 60^\circ
\]

Since \( \cos 60^\circ = 0.5 \), the work done is:

\[
W = 60.0 \times 10.0 \times 0.5 = 360 \text{ J}
\]

Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{360 \text{ J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

