","(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second law:  
\[
F = ma
\]  
The force on the proton is due to the electric field:  
\[
F = qE
\]  
Substituting \(f = qE\) into Newton's second law:  
\[
qE = ma
\]  
Solving for acceleration:  
\[
a = \frac{qE}{m} = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.81 \times 10^{8} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
Thus, the magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.81 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) The proton starts from rest, so its initial speed is \(v_0 = 0 \, \text{m/s}\). The acceleration is \(a = 4.81 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2\), and the desired velocity is \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\). Using the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = v_0 + at
\]  
Solving for time:  
\[
t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0 \, \text{m/s}}{4.81 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.27 \times 10^{-5} \, \text{s}
\]  
