","The decay length (\(\lambda_c\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:  
\[
\lambda_c = \frac{2\pi}{k_f}
\]  
where \(k_f\) is the free-field propagation angle (Fresel constant) of the incident light.  

The Fresnel constant is related to the difference in refractive indices:  
\[
\frac{n_{\text{eff}} - n_f}{n_{\text{eff}} + n_f} = \frac{n_{\text{air}} - n_{\text{film}}}{n_{\text{air}} + n_{\text{film}}}
\]  
where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective index of the slab, \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the cladding, and \(n_{\text{air}}\) is the refractive index of air (approximately 1).  

Given \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), we can rearrange the above equation to solve for \(n_{\text{eff}} + n_f\):  
\[
n_{\text{eff}} + n_f = \frac{2n_f}{1 - (n_{\text{eff}} - n_f)}
\]  
Substituting \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f = 0.3\):  
\[
n_{\text{eff}} + n_f = \frac{2n_f}{1 - 0.3} = \frac{2n_f}{0.7} \approx 2.857n_f
\]  
The refractive index of GaAs is approximately \(1.42\), and the refractive index of Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)As is approximately \(1.52\). Since the cladding is Ga\(_{02}\)Al\(_{.8}\)As, we use \(n_f = 1.52\):  
\[
n_{\text{eff}} + n_f = 2.857 \times 1.52 \approx 4.34
\]  
Now, we can find \(n_{\text{eff}}\):  
\[
n_{\text{eff}} = 4.34 - 1.52 \approx 2.82
\]  
Thus, \(n_{\text{eff}} \approx 2.82\) and \(n_f \approx 1.52\).  

The dispersion constant \(k_f\) is related to the wavelength (\(\lambda\)) and the refractive index (\(n\)) by:  
\[
k_f = \frac{2\pi}{n}
\]  
For the cladding, \(n_f = 1.52\), so:  
\[
k_f = \frac{2\pi}{1.52} \approx 4.14
\]  
The decay length is:  
\[
\lambda_c = \frac{2\pi}{k_f} = \frac{2\pi}{4.14} \approx 1.91 \text{ nm}
\]  

Thus, the decay length is \(\boxed{1.91 \text{ nm}}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
