<start_of_turn>model
","To compute the curl of a vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y) = \mathbf{R}(x, y)\), we use the cross product formula for the curl:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{R} = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \mathbf{k} \\R_x(x, y) & R_y(x, y) & 0\end{vmatrix}.
\]
Here, \(R_x = \frac{\partial}{\partial x} V_x(x, y)\) and \(R_y = \frac{\partial}{\partial y} V_y(x, y)\). The cross product simplifies to:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{R} = \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y} V_z(x, y) - R_x(x, y) \frac{\partial}{\partial y} V_y(x, y) \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} V_z(x, y) - R_x(x, y) \frac{\partial}{\partial x} V_y(x, y) \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} R_y(x, y) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} R_x(x, y) \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
Given that \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\) is a vector field that depends only on \(x\) and \(y\), it can be written as \(\mathbf{V}(x, y) = \mathbf{r}(x, y)\). The cross product simplifies to:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \begin{vvmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ \frac{\partial}{\partial x} & \frac{\partial}{\partial y} & \mathbf{k} \\V_x(x, y) & V_y(x, y) & 0\end{vmatrix}.
\]
Expanding the cross product gives:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = \left( 0 - V_z(x, y) \frac{\partial}{\partial y} V_y(x, y) \right) \mathbf{i} - \left( 0 - V_z(x, y) \frac{\partial}{\partial x} V_y(x, y) \right) \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial x} V_y(x, y) - \frac{\partial}{\partial y} V_x(x, y) \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
However, since \(\mathbf{V}\) is only a function of \(x\) and \(y\), the \(z\)-component is zero, so \(V_z = 0\). Thus, the cross product simplifies to:
\[
\nabla \times \mathbf{V} = 0 \mathbf{i} - 0 \mathbf{j} + \left( \frac{\partial V_y(x, y)}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial V_x(x, y)}{\partial y} \right) \mathbf{k}.
\]
